<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward I of England</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1239–<a href="/years/1307">1307</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Edward Longshanks</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward I was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> from 1272 to 1307. He conquered the principality of Gwynedd in the wars of 1277 and 1282–1283, after which he secured north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> with castles such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caernarfon">Caernarfon</a>, Conwy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harlech">Harlech</a>, and he later fought long wars to subdue <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. His reign also saw major legal reforms and the growth of Parliament.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england.jpg" alt="Edward I of England" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_I_-_Westminster_Abbey_Sedilia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Yet <b>Edward I</b>’s campaigns of 1277 and 1282–1283 showed the limits of Welsh power: English armies, supplied by sea, seized the harvest of Anglesey and slowly encircled Snowdonia, despite a Welsh victory at Moel-y-don on the Menai Strait in November 1282.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-gwynedd">Kingdom of Gwynedd · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Wars of Independence began when <b>Edward I of England</b> invaded in 1296, sacked Berwick and took the Stone of Scone to Westminster.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Philip II of France expelled them from his lands in 1182 and recalled them in 1198; <b>Edward I of England</b> expelled all Jews from his kingdom in 1290, and Philip IV of France did the same in 1306.</blockquote><a href="/societies/feudal-europe">Social Hierarchy of Feudal Europe</a></li><li><blockquote>The second Treaty of Birgham, under which Margaret, Maid of Norway, was to marry Edward of Caernarfon, son of <b>Edward I of England</b>, was ratified at Northampton.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-28">28 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>King <b>Edward I of England</b> sacked Berwick-upon-Tweed, then the richest Scottish burgh and the kingdom's main port for wool exports.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-30">30 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Noblemen headed by <b>Edward I of England</b> chose John Balliol as King of Scots over Robert de Brus, ending the succession dispute known as the Great Cause.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-17">17 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
